-- MAGIC #### A2.1 Invalid Reasons for Multiple Metastores per Region
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC Despite Databricks recommendations and processes encouraging the one metastore/region model, some customers mistakenly believe they need more than one metastore per region due to misconceptions about how Unity Catalog works or uncertainty about their governance requirements. Here are common false reasons for requesting multiple metastores, and clarification of why these concerns are unfounded:
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC - **Multiple workspaces:** belief that separate metastores are needed for different workspaces. In reality, Unity Catalog is designed to unify governance across multiple workspaces within a region, allowing secure, role-based sharing and permission management without siloing metadata.
-- MAGIC - **Data isolation requirements:** concern that data access conflicts between teams require isolation via separate metastores. Unity Catalog supports fine-grained access controls within a single metastore, so teams and business units can securely share or segregate data without duplicating governance infrastructure.
-- MAGIC - **DevOps requirements:** assumption that production and non-production environments cannot be managed together. As mentioned in the previous point, Unity Catalog enables logical separation through catalogs, schemas, and permission boundaries, eliminating the need for physical separation at the metastore level for most scenarios.
-- MAGIC - **Load balancing:** fear that performance or scalability issues require multiple metastores to distribute the load. Unity Catalog is optimized to maintain metadata performance and scale within a single metastore for most enterprise needs. Capacity concerns can be managed without unnecessary fragmentation.
-- MAGIC - **Multiple subscriptions/accounts:** belief that each cloud subscription/account requires its own metastore. Unity Catalog can map multiple cloud accounts to a single regional metastore, maintaining governance and visibility without extra administrative overhead.

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md
-- MAGIC ## B. Catalog Layout
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC Catalog naming conventions in Unity Catalog play an important role in organizing, isolating, and securing data for a variety of business and technical use cases.

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md
-- MAGIC ### B1. Catalog Naming
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC Catalog naming conventions in Unity Catalog play an important role in organizing, isolating, and securing data for a variety of business and technical use cases. 
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC The most important aspect of catalog naming is adopting a clear, consistent, and hierarchical scheme that supports governance, discoverability, and operational efficiency. Focus on lowercase names with full words that encode environments and business domains, or whatever meets your organizational requirements, in catalog names.
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC Why this matters:
-- MAGIC - **Longevity and contracts:** names are long-lived identifiers and act as contracts within your data platform. Changing them can break dependencies and integrations
-- MAGIC - **Developer efficiency:** consistent naming reduces cognitive load and helps developers quickly identify the purpose and ownership of catalogs

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md-sandbox
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC #### B1.1 Naming Use Cases
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC Careful naming and organization of catalogs can be used to achieve the following:
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC - **Environment Isolation:** separate environments for development, testing, and production help isolate storage, compute, and administrative access
-- MAGIC - **Business Unit Isolation:** in larger organizations, business units or departments get their own catalogs, doubly ensuring data is segregated for access management, governance, and compliance. Each unit can work independently within their catalog, enabling tailored governance and self-serve publishing
-- MAGIC - **Central Governance:** central teams can maintain naming conventions and rules for published/shared catalogs, where other teams' data can be published after quality checks. This supports discoverability and auditing.
-- MAGIC - **Access Control Use Case:** catalog workspace binding enables restricting catalog access to specific workspaces
-- MAGIC - **Multi-region and Multi-cloud Scenarios:** when crossing regions or clouds, catalogs may follow conventions that include a region name to make their context and scope clear
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC <link rel="stylesheet" href="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/picnic">
-- MAGIC

-- COMMAND ----------

