-- MAGIC %md-sandbox
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC ### B3. Downscoping with Catalog Binding
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC By default, catalogs are open to all workspaces attached to the same metastore. Access to those catalogs is subject to the access rules (and the principal doing the accessing).
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC Workspace binding is a core mechanism for enforcing data isolation across environments and teams. It allows administrators to bind catalogs to specific workspaces, so only those workspaces can access the bound resources.
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC This provides a coarse-grained, "harder" access boundary layer on top of Unity Catalog’s existing fine-grained access controls. 
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC If a catalog is not bound to a workspace, a principal would be denied access even if they have access to objects within it. In this scenario, the only way to access those objects is through a workspace to which the catalog is bound.
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC The diagram below illustrates this mechanism in action. Catalog collocation and permissions notwithstanding, binding enforces the following constraints on top of everything else:
-- MAGIC - Even though members in the *sales* group have access rights on both *sales_dev* and *sales_prd* catalogs:
-- MAGIC    - they can only access items in the *sales_dev* catalog from the *sales_dev* workspace (subject to any additional fine-grained permissions)
-- MAGIC    - they can only access items in the *sales_prd* catalog from the *sales_prd* workspace (subject to any additional fine-grained permissions)
-- MAGIC
